diagnosis_text:
I tested end-to-end connectivity from the user’s PC (pc5, 172.16.10.5) to the design group hosts (pc12–pc14, 172.16.30.x). All probes succeed consistently: pc5 can ping 172.16.30.12/.13/.14 with 0% loss and ~1–2 ms RTT. The replies show TTL=63, indicating exactly one routed hop, which confirms the inter-VLAN gateway between 10.x and 30.x is up and forwarding. In the reverse direction, the topology-wide reachability check shows 172.16.30.x hosts can also reach 172.16.10.5 with no loss. 

This rules out a broken uplink or trunk between floors (switch1 trunk -> mswitch0 -> switch3), and it rules out any inter-VLAN routing failure. The L2 access segments (VLAN10 on switch1 ports for pc5–pc8 and VLAN30 on switch3 ports for pc12–pc14) are passing traffic, and the routed path between them is healthy.

Localization: No faulty device found. Verified healthy path across switch1 (eth1 trunk_sw1_0_2), mswitch0 (inter-switch links), and switch3 (eth0 uplink), with the inter-VLAN router correctly decrementing TTL by one.

Root cause: Not reproducible; there is no current network-side fault between the 172.16.10.0/24 and 172.16.30.0/24 groups.

Corrective fix: None required on the network. Please retry from the user’s session. If issues persist, check the endpoint (correct IPs, local firewall, or stale ARP/NIC state) and share a timestamp and target IP so we can correlate logs, but the campus network path is currently operating normally. 
